<p>Hail damage claims in San Marcos tend to arrive in waves. One spring storm can track along I-35 and leave pocked shingles, split window screens, and dented gutters across entire subdivisions, and then every roofer and insurance adjuster in the region is booked for weeks. As a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a>, our job is to slow the process down enough to get the documentation right: when the storm hit, what it damaged, and what a proper repair actually costs.</p>
<p>This page explains how we approach hail claims for homes and businesses in San Marcos, Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, and the rest of Hays County, what tends to cause disagreements with insurers, and when it makes sense to ask for a second set of eyes.</p>

<h2>Hail in Hays County: What the Weather Record Shows</h2>
<p>The NOAA Storm Events Database lists 180 hail reports for Hays County from 1967 through 2026. Fifty-eight of those were golf-ball size (1.75 inches) or larger, and eight reached baseball size (2.75 inches) or more. May accounts for the most reports, with April close behind, so spring is when most local hail claims begin.</p>
