<h2>Common Disputes on San Marcos Hail Damage Claims</h2>
<h3>Wear and tear versus hail</h3>
<p>Central Texas sun is hard on asphalt shingles, and insurers often attribute granule loss or cracking to age. The difference between weathering and a hail strike is usually visible up close, but it has to be photographed and explained, not just asserted.</p>
<h3>Cosmetic damage exclusions on metal roofs</h3>
<p>Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs are common on Hill Country homes, barns, and shops west of San Marcos. Some policies exclude dents that are only cosmetic. Whether a particular dent affects water-shedding, seam integrity, or fasteners is often the core of the disagreement.</p>
<h3>Percentage deductibles</h3>
<p>A 1% or 2% wind/hail deductible on a home insured for several hundred thousand dollars can be larger than many policyholders expect. Knowing the number before the inspection helps you decide whether a claim makes sense.</p>
<h3>Repair versus replacement</h3>
<p>Insurers sometimes scope a few slopes or individual shingles for repair. Whether full replacement is supported can depend on how widespread the damage is, whether matching materials are available, and the policy language. For roof-specific issues, see our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>How the Hail Claim Process Works With Us</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Free claim review.</strong> We talk through what happened, look at your policy and any insurer paperwork, and tell you plainly whether we think we can help.</li>
<li><strong>Written agreement.</strong> If you hire us, the fee and terms are set out in a TDI-compliant contract you can rescind within 72 hours.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection and storm dating.</strong> We inspect the roof and exterior, document damage, and match it to NOAA and NWS storm data.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate and submission.</strong> We prepare a detailed estimate and send it to the insurer with supporting photos and records.</li>
<li><strong>Follow-up and negotiation.</strong> We respond to requests, meet the carrier&rsquo;s adjuster or engineer on site when needed, and pursue <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> for items found during repairs.</li>
</ol>
<p>Texas prompt-payment rules give insurers set timeframes to acknowledge, investigate, and decide claims, though those deadlines can be extended after a declared weather catastrophe. If your claim has stalled, our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed claims</a> page explains what is typical.</p>

<h2>When to Call a Public Adjuster About Hail</h2>
<p>Many small hail claims can be handled directly with the insurer. It may be worth calling a public adjuster in San Marcos when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The insurer&rsquo;s estimate is close to or below your deductible, yet you can see damage on multiple slopes or elevations.</li>
<li>The claim was denied as wear and tear, cosmetic, or pre-existing. Our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied claims</a> page covers next steps.</li>
<li>Your property has a metal roof, multiple structures, or commercial roofing that is harder to scope.</li>
<li>Hail also damaged windows, siding, and equipment that the first estimate left out.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are not sure where your claim stands, a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> is a low-pressure way to find out. Our San Marcos Public Adjuster team also handles <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> when the same storm brings both.</p>
